activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T01:07:09.5527307Z 6. After calling `create_pull_request`, STOP. Do not probe with another PR call, do not test auth with manual `git push`, and do not rewrite git remotes.
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T01:07:09.5528295Z 
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T01:07:09.5530975Z **Important**: The `branch` parameter in the create_pull_request tool **must exactly match the name of your current local git branch** — the branch you just committed to. You can verify this with `git branch --show-current`. Never invent or guess a branch name; always use the actual branch name from `git branch --show-current`. If you are on an existing branch (e.g. you checked out a PR branch), use that branch name.
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T01:07:09.5533556Z 
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T01:07:09.5535160Z **Important**: `create_pull_request` is not a sandbox. Every successful call records a real PR intent that can become an externally visible pull request. Never use placeholder payloads like `"test"` / `"test"` to see what happens. If you are not ready to open the real PR, use `noop` or `report_incomplete` instead.
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T01:07:09.5537131Z </safe-output-tools>
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T01:07:09.5537590Z <mcp-clis>
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T01:07:09.5538632Z The following servers are available as CLI executables on `PATH`. Invoke them from bash - they are **not** MCP tools; do not call them via an MCP tool interface.
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T01:07:09.5539616Z 
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T01:07:09.5540014Z - `safeoutputs` — run `safeoutputs --help` to see available tools
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T01:07:09.5540514Z 
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T01:07:09.5540871Z For `safeoutputs` and `mcpscripts`, always use the CLI commands above.
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T01:07:09.5541397Z 
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T01:07:09.5542692Z For `safeoutputs`, every successful call is a real write-intent declaration - do not use it for probing, auth checks, or placeholder payloads. Use `noop` or `report_incomplete` if not ready to emit the final action.
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T01:07:09.5543969Z 
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T01:07:09.5544428Z For multiple or complex arguments, pipe a JSON object on stdin using `.` as the sentinel:
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T01:07:09.5545254Z ```bash
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T01:07:09.5545868Z printf '{"item_number":42,"body":"### Title\n\nBody."}' | safeoutputs add_comment .
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T01:07:09.5546810Z # or write to a file: safeoutputs create_pull_request . < /tmp/payload.json
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T01:07:09.5547519Z ```
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T01:07:09.5547732Z 
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T01:07:09.5548349Z To inject an entire local file as the `body` field without re-embedding its content in the model context, use `jq -Rs`:
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T01:07:09.5549286Z ```bash
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T01:07:09.5549766Z jq -Rs --arg discussion_number "$DISCUSSION_NUMBER" \
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T01:07:09.5550503Z   '{discussion_number: ($discussion_number|tonumber), body: .}' \
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T01:07:09.5551179Z   discussion-body.md \
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T01:07:09.5551671Z   | safeoutputs update_discussion .
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T01:07:09.5552348Z ```
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T01:07:09.5553649Z `jq -Rs` reads the file as a raw string (`-R`) and slurps it into a single JSON string value (`-s`), so `body` is always a valid JSON field. Piping `cat file | safeoutputs ...` does not populate `body` and will be rejected.
